              <span class="lights"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
              <span class="btab">
                <span class="favicon"><img src="/favicon.svg" alt="" width="14" height="14" /><i></i></span>
                tabsh
                <span class="mark">×</span>
              </span>
              <!-- The rest of the workday, in the tabs next door. -->
              <span class="btab bg">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="currentColor"><path d="M12 .3a12 12 0 0 0-3.8 23.4c.6.1.8-.3.8-.6v-2c-3.3.7-4-1.6-4-1.6-.6-1.4-1.4-1.8-1.4-1.8-1-.7.1-.7.1-.7 1.2.1 1.8 1.2 1.8 1.2 1.1 1.8 2.8 1.3 3.5 1 0-.8.4-1.3.7-1.6-2.7-.3-5.5-1.3-5.5-5.9 0-1.3.5-2.4 1.2-3.2 0-.3-.5-1.5.2-3.2 0 0 1-.3 3.3 1.2a11.5 11.5 0 0 1 6 0C17.3 4.7 18.3 5 18.3 5c.7 1.7.2 2.9.1 3.2.8.8 1.2 1.9 1.2 3.2 0 4.6-2.8 5.6-5.5 5.9.4.4.8 1.1.8 2.2v3.3c0 .3.2.7.8.6A12 12 0 0 0 12 .3"/></svg>
                <span class="label">Fix refund status · #142</span>
              </span>
              <span class="btab bg">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke-width="4.2" stroke-linecap="round">
                  <path stroke="#36c5f0" d="M3.5 9.5h6M9.5 3.5h0" /><path stroke="#2eb67d" d="M14.5 3.5v6M20.5 9.5h0" />
                  <path stroke="#ecb22e" d="M14.5 14.5h6M14.5 20.5h0" /><path stroke="#e01e5a" d="M9.5 14.5v6M3.5 14.5h0" />
                </svg>
                <span class="label">#shop-eng</span>
              </span>
              <span class="btab bg">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9.5" /><path d="M2.5 12h19M12 2.5c3 3.2 3 15.8 0 19M12 2.5c-3 3.2-3 15.8 0 19" /></svg>
                <span class="label">Shop · localhost:5173</span>
              </span>
            </div>
            <div class="urlbar" aria-hidden="true"><span class="url">tabsh.cc/app/?view=board&layout=list&drawer=1</span></div>
            <!-- The real app, on a pretend daemon in memory (src/demo/daemon.ts).
                 Held until the poster is clicked, so the landing page does not boot it. -->
            <div class="app">
              <button type="button" class="poster" data-run>
                <!-- One still, chosen here so the other file is never requested. -->
                <img alt="" decoding="async" data-light="/preview-light.png?v=7" data-dark="/preview-dark.png?v=7" />
                <script is:inline>
                  const still = document.currentScript.previousElementSibling;
                  const scheme = matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: light)');
                  const pick = () => (scheme.matches ? still.dataset.light : still.dataset.dark);
                  still.src = pick();
                  scheme.addEventListener('change', () => (still.src = pick()));
                </script>
                <span>Run the live demo</span>
              </button>
              <iframe data-click data-src="/demo/?view=board&layout=list&tab=demo-9&drawer=1" title="tabsh demo: the board with a card's chat open"></iframe>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="browser"><span class="mark">##</span> WHY A BROWSER TAB?</h2>
      <ul class="steps">
        <li>
          <b>Your work already happens in the browser.</b> The issue, the pull
          request, the docs, the design, your team's chat, the AI you ask for
          help and the app you're building on localhost are all tabs. The
          terminal is the last tool still in a window of its own.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Your apps already work great in a tab.</b> Other tools pull them
          into their own window instead: a built-in browser, a pull request
          panel, a chat pane. Each copy is a little worse than the real thing,
          without your extensions or your logins, so you end up keeping both.
          tabsh leaves GitHub as GitHub and Slack as Slack, and makes the shell
          one more tab.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Attention is scarce, and app switching kills it.</b> Every ⌘-Tab
          out to a terminal window costs you your train of thought. In tabsh the
          terminal sits in the tab next to the thing it builds.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="list"><span class="mark">##</span> ONE LIST, EVERY PROJECT</h2>
      <p>
        Columns move one card. The list catches you up: every card, every
        project, one scroll, grouped by status and tagged with its folder. Open
        a card and its shell is right there.
      </p>
      <div class="demo-pair">
        <figure class="still-fig">
          <picture>
            <source srcset="/shots/list-terminal-dark.png" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
            <img
              src="/shots/list-terminal-light.png"
              alt="The tabsh list beside a live terminal: cards from the shop and api projects grouped by status on the left, the shop: npm run dev shell on the right."
              width="2880"
              height="1800"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
          </picture>
          <figcaption class="caption"><span class="mark">fig. 2</span> the list, with a shell beside it.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="still-fig">
          <picture>
            <source srcset="/shots/board-dark.png" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
            <img
              src="/shots/board-light.png"
              alt="The tabsh board as columns: Backlog, In progress, Needs input, Completed and Archived, with cards from the shop and api projects."
              width="2880"
              height="1800"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
          </picture>
          <figcaption class="caption"><span class="mark">fig. 3</span> the same cards as a board, when you want to drag one across.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <b>Every project, one place.</b> A shop card and an api card, one
          list. Each card keeps its folder.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Grouped, not scattered.</b> Backlog, In progress, Needs input,
          Completed, Archived. Fold what's done.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>One scroll.</b> No columns to pan, no drawer to open. If a card
          needs you, it's already there.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="agents"><span class="mark">##</span> YOUR AGENTS, YOUR CALL</h2>
      <p>
        Pick the agent per card: Claude Code, Codex, Cursor CLI, OpenCode, or
        any CLI that takes a prompt. Run it as a terminal (TUI) or a chat (UI);
        switch per card. Models change in the chat.
      </p>
      <figure class="agent-pair">
        <div class="agent-panel">
          <p class="agent-label"><b>TUI</b> Claude Code in a real terminal</p>
          <picture>
            <source srcset="/shots/tui-panel-dark.png" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
            <img
              src="/shots/tui-panel-light.png"
              alt="Claude Code running as a terminal in a tabsh card: its refund transcript, tool lines, test result, and a prompt asking whether to commit."
              width="1440"
              height="1800"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
          </picture>
        </div>
        <div class="agent-panel">
          <p class="agent-label"><b>UI</b> Claude Code as a chat</p>
          <picture>
            <source srcset="/shots/ui-panel-dark.png" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
            <img
              src="/shots/ui-panel-light.png"
              alt="Claude Code running as a chat in a tabsh card: the refund conversation, the message composer, and the Claude Sonnet 4.5 model picker."
              width="1440"
              height="1800"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
          </picture>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="caption"><span class="mark">fig. 4</span> one agent, two surfaces: a real terminal (TUI, left) or a chat (UI, right).</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Providers are commands. Pick one to set its startup, resume and ACP
        commands, or add any CLI that takes a prompt. <code>{'{prompt}'}</code>{' '}
        is the card's first prompt; <code>{'{session}'}</code> is a resumable
        conversation.
      </p>
      <figure class="shot still-fig provider-flow">
        <picture>
          <source srcset="/shots/providers-dark.png" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
          <img
            src="/shots/providers-light.png"
            alt="The Agent providers list in tabsh's command palette: Claude Code, Codex, Cursor CLI and OpenCode, each with its startup command, and an Add provider row."
            width="1248"
            height="664"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </picture>
        <picture>
          <source srcset="/shots/providers-detail-dark.png" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
          <img
            src="/shots/providers-detail-light.png"
            alt="Claude Code's provider settings in tabsh: its name, startup command, resume command and ACP command."
            width="1248"
            height="664"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </picture>
        <figcaption class="caption"><span class="mark">fig. 5</span> pick a provider, then set its startup, resume and ACP commands.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        There is no worktree choreography. Agents run in the folder you started
        them in, on your branch. That is the trade: no isolation, so two agents
        in the same checkout can collide. tabsh doesn't hide that behind a copy
        of your repo.
      </p>

      <h2 id="remote"><span class="mark">##</span> OPTIONAL: ANOTHER DEVICE</h2>
      <p>
        The daemon listens on loopback. If you want the same board on your phone
        or another laptop, tabsh can serve itself over Tailscale: turn it on,
        scan the code, and the device is paired. Off by default, and there is no
        relay of ours in between.
      </p>
      <figure class="shot still-fig">
        <picture>
          <source srcset="/shots/tailscale-dark.png" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
          <img
            src="/shots/tailscale-light.png"
            alt="The Open on another device page in tabsh: Tailscale sharing is on, with a QR code for a new device and a list of paired devices."
            width="2880"
            height="1800"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </picture>
        <figcaption class="caption"><span class="mark">fig. 6</span> optional: serve the same board to a phone over Tailscale.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="features"><span class="mark">##</span> FEATURES</h2>
      <pre class="table" data-rows={JSON.stringify(features)}>{table}</pre>
      <p class="muted"><span class="mark">&gt;</span> Keys are the Mac defaults. Change any of them from ⌘K.</p>

      <h2 id="faq"><span class="mark">##</span> FAQ</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What's the difference between the board and the list?</summary>
          <p>
            The board is columns you drag cards across. The list is the same cards in one scroll,
            grouped by status. ⌘B switches between them; the list is the one for catching up.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does it work in Safari?</summary>
          <p>
            Safari (and every iOS browser) won't let this site reach the daemon, so the daemon serves
            the app itself at <code>http://tabsh.localhost:7681</code>, and this site sends Safari there.
            File → Add to Dock opens it like an app.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Why does Chrome ask to access apps on my device?</summary>
          <p>That's the connection to the daemon on your machine. Allow it once.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is it safe?</summary>
          <p>
            The daemon listens on 127.0.0.1 only and answers only the tabsh app carrying your token. The
            token rides in the URL fragment, which browsers never send to a server. Keystrokes never
            leave your machine.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I open it from my phone or another computer?</summary>
          <p>
            Keep the daemon on loopback — exposing the port on the network would be plain HTTP to a
            shell. If you need another of your devices, put Tailscale (Serve or an SSH tunnel) in
            front of it. Use the Tailscale IP, not MagicDNS. <a href="/docs/#tailscale">How to set that up</a>.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can it keep running after I close the terminal?</summary>
          <p>
            With Homebrew, <code>brew services start tabsh</code> runs it from login. Then open
            <code>{DAEMON_URL}</code> once to pair this browser.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which systems does it run on?</summary>
          <p>macOS and Linux. There's no Windows build.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How do I upgrade?</summary>
          <p>
            With Homebrew: <code>brew upgrade tabsh</code>, then <code>brew services restart tabsh</code> if you run it as a service.
            With the install script: re-run it.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Lost the link?</summary>
          <p>Open <code>{DAEMON_URL}</code> in the same browser and the daemon sends you back to the app, paired.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is it free?</summary>
          <p>Yes. It's open source under the MIT license, on <a href={REPO_URL}>GitHub</a>.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

      <footer>
        <p class="ph"><ProductHuntBadge /></p>
        <p>
          <span class="mark">&gt;</span> Get it: <a href="/docs/">install tabsh</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Built with care by <a href="https://blaisetiong.dev">Blaise</a>.
        </p>
      </footer>

      <p class="sig end">~*~ EOF ~*~</p>

  <Fragment slot="after">

    <script>
      // Stretch the ASCII table to the column: measure one character, then pad
      // the second column with however many fit. Never narrower than the content.
      const tablePre = document.querySelector<HTMLPreElement>('.table');
      if (tablePre) {
        const rows: string[][] = JSON.parse(tablePre.dataset.rows ?? '[]');
        const widths = rows[0].map((_, i) => Math.max(...rows.map((r) => r[i].length)));
        const probe = document.createElement('span');
        probe.textContent = 'x'.repeat(100);
        probe.style.cssText = 'position:absolute;visibility:hidden';
        const draw = () => {
          tablePre.append(probe);
          const charWidth = probe.getBoundingClientRect().width / 100;
          probe.remove();
          const fit = Math.floor(tablePre.clientWidth / charWidth);
          // Each column costs its width plus 3 (a border and a space either side), plus the last border.
          const others = widths.reduce((sum, w, i) => (i === 1 ? sum : sum + w + 3), 1);
          const w = widths.map((x, i) => (i === 1 ? Math.max(x, fit - others - 3) : x));
          const rule = `+${w.map((x) => '-'.repeat(x + 2)).join('+')}+`;
          const line = (row: string[]) => `|${row.map((c, i) => ` ${c.padEnd(w[i])} `).join('|')}|`;
          tablePre.textContent = [rule, ...rows.map(line), rule].join('\n');
        };
        document.fonts.ready.then(() => {
          draw();
          new ResizeObserver(draw).observe(tablePre);
        });
      }

      // Fig. 1 waits for a click, so the landing page does not boot the demo.
      for (const poster of document.querySelectorAll('[data-run]')) {
        poster.addEventListener('click', () => {
          const frame = poster.parentElement?.querySelector('iframe[data-src]');
          if (!(frame instanceof HTMLIFrameElement) || frame.getAttribute('src')) return;
          frame.src = frame.dataset.src ?? '';
          poster.remove();
        });
      }

      for (const btn of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy]')) {
        btn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
          await navigator.clipboard.writeText(btn.dataset.copy ?? '');
          btn.textContent = '[copied]';
          setTimeout(() => (btn.textContent = '[copy]'), 1500);
        });
      }
    </script>
  </Fragment>
</Site>

<style>
  /* fig. 1: a browser window, drawn in the tabsh palette, framing the real app. */
  .browser {
    --border: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg) 14%, var(--bg));
    --chrome: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg) 5%, var(--bg));
    overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 10px; background: var(--bg);
    font: 13px/1.5 'Geist', ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
    /* A laptop screen's 16:10; the app takes whatever height is left. */
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; aspect-ratio: 16 / 10;
  }
  .chrome { display: flex; align-items: flex-end; gap: .75rem; padding: .25rem .75rem 0; background: var(--chrome); }
  .lights { display: flex; gap: 6px; align-self: center; padding-bottom: .375rem; }
  .lights i { width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--border); }
  .btab {
    display: flex; flex-shrink: 0; align-items: center; gap: .5rem; width: 12rem; max-width: 60%;
    padding: .375rem .625rem; border-radius: 8px 8px 0 0; background: var(--bg);
  }
  .btab .mark { margin-left: auto; }
  /* Background tabs: no fill, dimmer, split by hairlines like a real tab strip. */
  .btab.bg {
    position: relative; flex: 0 1 12rem; width: auto; min-width: 0; background: none; color: var(--muted);
    white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }
  .btab.bg svg { flex-shrink: 0; }
  .btab.bg .label { min-width: 0; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; }
  .btab.bg + .btab.bg::before {
    content: ""; position: absolute; left: -.375rem; top: 25%; bottom: 25%; width: 1px; background: var(--border);
  }
  @media (max-width: 720px) { .btab.bg:nth-of-type(n+4) { display: none; } }
  @media (max-width: 520px) { .btab.bg { display: none; } }
  .favicon { position: relative; display: flex; }
  .favicon img { border-radius: 3px; }
  /* claude's tab wants you, so the tab's favicon carries the app's badge. */
  .favicon i { position: absolute; top: -3px; right: -3px; width: 7px; height: 7px; border-radius: 50%; background: #f97316; box-shadow: 0 0 0 1.5px var(--bg); }
  .urlbar { padding: .375rem .75rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .url { display: block; padding: .125rem .75rem; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--chrome); color: var(--muted); overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap; }
  .app { position: relative; flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
  /* Fig. 1 floats on a painted desktop. The still stands in until a click. */
  .stage {
    padding: 2.75rem 3.25rem 3.5rem; border-radius: 4px;
    background: #c4b49a url('/preview-ground.jpg') center / cover no-repeat;
  }
  .stage .browser {
    border-color: rgb(48 38 28 / 32%);
    box-shadow: 0 16px 36px rgb(28 22 16 / 18%);
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: light) {
    .stage .browser {
      --bg: #efeae4;
      --chrome: #e7e1d8;
      --border: rgb(72 58 44 / 22%);
      border-color: rgb(62 50 38 / 28%);
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) { .stage { padding: 1rem .85rem 1.25rem; } }
  .poster {
    position: absolute; z-index: 1; inset: 0; display: block; width: 100%; height: 100%;
    padding: 0; border: 0; cursor: pointer; background: #f7f7f7;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { .poster { background: #141414; } }
  .poster img { display: block; width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; object-position: top left; }
  .poster span {
    position: absolute; left: 50%; top: 50%; transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    padding: .4rem .8rem; border-radius: 999px; background: rgb(20 20 20 / 90%); color: #f4f4f4;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px rgb(255 255 255 / 55%);
    font: 500 13px/1 'Geist', ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif; white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .poster:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--link); outline-offset: -4px; }
  .app iframe { display: block; width: 100%; height: 100%; border: 0; }
  /* 16:10 is too short to use on a phone. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) { .browser { aspect-ratio: auto; } .app { flex: none; height: 28rem; } .lights { display: none; } }
</style>

<style>
  .table { white-space: pre; }

  footer { margin-top: 3rem; }
  .ph { margin: 0 0 1rem; }

  /* FAQ: each question a [+] line that opens to [-]. */
  .faq { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .faq details + details { margin-top: .25rem; }
  .faq summary { cursor: pointer; list-style: none; padding-left: 4ch; text-indent: -4ch; }
